    {
      "title": "Govt amends motor vehicle rules to allow flex-fuel vehicles running on E100",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/industry/auto/govt-amends-motor-vehicle-rules-to-allow-flex-fuel-vehicles-running-on-e100-126102600763_1.html",
      "description": "The Road Transport Ministry on Monday notified changes to the Central Motor Vehicles Rules, allowing flex-fuel vehicles that can run on up to E100 (nearly pure ethanol).\nE100 fuel allows vehicles to run on nearly pure ethanol.\nA senior road transport ministry official said fuel beyond E20 (20 per cent ethanol-blended petrol) and up to E100 is intended only for vehicles designed and certified to use it and will not be applicable for other vehicles. Vehicles that use E20 or normal fuel can't use E85 or E100 fuel.\nThe Ministry of Road Transport and Highways (MoRTH) has changed certain vehicle classification thresholds, fuel specifications, and technical references used in emission-related tables and testing provisions.\nAdditionally, the government has raised the vehicle weight limit to 3,500 kg from the previous threshold of 3,000 kg for light petrol and diesel vehicles affected by these changes.\nIn June this year, Maruti Suzuki India launched India's first flex-fuel passenger vehicl",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-10-26T10:29:59"
    }
  ]
